A||||3||Eric||clutch problems
Z||000000||Eric||05-04-99||06:06 PM||||My 88 GT is dead.  Well, almost.  It may as well be dead if I can't drive the damn thing.  When I bought the car, a few months ago, I noticed the shifting effort was way too high. (I may have just been comparing it to my old Celica "wuss car" with zero clutch pedal effort)  Unfortunatly, the problem worsened.  I decided to bleed the clutch.  This worked for only a short time and then I was back to grinding gears.  I replaced the master and slave cylinders to no avail.  After a weekend of bleeding and driving, and bleeding and driving, I found a leak in the braided stainless line that attaches to the slave cylinder.  For the past week, I have been trying to fabricate a new hose.  I tried rubber brake hose, but it busted on me.  Then I went to Jegs.  I was hoping they would be willing to help me, but they basically farted me off.  I was informed that the slave cylinder took a metric fitting and wouldn't work with any components they stock at Jegs.  I searched the yellow pages for an answer, but found nothing.  I need some suggestions for making a new braided stainless line from someone who has done this before.  By the way, I cut the hard line about an inch back from the start of the hose.  Thanks.
Z||000001||PaUL||05-04-99||07:17 PM||||Eric,<br>Please read an earlier post called "clutch info uncovered" goes into detail and theories from different people me included on clutch failure. <p>Check out my Unorthodox Clutch Fix.
Z||000002||PaUL||05-04-99||07:20 PM||||You can buy a tube flaring kit and fittings to make your own hose and connections. 
Z||000003||Eric||05-05-99||02:09 PM||||Thanks Paul.  I put the extra slave piston into my new slave cylinder.  It seemed to fit well and hopefully it will work like you say it does.  I could really stand to lose a couple inches of clutch travel.  I feel like I'm starting to kick my way through the wheel well.<p>Where can I get this line and fitting "kit" you mentioned?
